CORE
main.c
--unwind 4 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^VERIFICATION INCONCLUSIVE$
^SIGNAL=0$
^No property was generated and the run was truncated
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
An empty property set was folded into a proof.

determine_result() starts from PASS and conjoins the status of every
property, so when the set is *empty* the fold yields PASS and the tool prints
VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL having checked nothing whatsoever.

A setup loop longer than the unwind bound produces exactly that. The
unwinding assumption makes every path past the bound infeasible, so execution
stops inside the loop, never reaches the code under test, and no property is
generated. Here 64 array elements are written before the thread is created;
at --unwind 4 the race below is not merely missed, it is never expressed.

The real instance is `pthread/indexer`, whose main initialises 128 mutexes
before creating any thread:

    --unwind 32   SUCCESSFUL   no verification condition   unwinding truncated
    --unwind 129  (timeout)    no verification condition   unwinding complete

It is expected-false, so that SUCCESSFUL scored -32 where silence scores 0.
Climbing the bound does not help and that was the confusing part: every bound
up to 32 gives the same vacuous answer, and the first one that would say
anything is 129.

An empty property set is not wrong on its own -- a program with no shared
access genuinely has nothing to check under --datarace, and must still be
SUCCESSFUL. It is empty *and truncated* that cannot be a proof, so the marker
is only recorded when `unwinding_incomplete` or `thread_creation_bound_hit`
is set.

It is recorded on the early-return path rather than in update_properties,
where a first attempt put it: on UNSAT,
`update_properties_status_from_goals` turns every UNKNOWN property into PASS,
including one that has no goal in the decider at all, so the marker was
flipped straight back and the verdict stayed SUCCESSFUL while the diagnostic
printed. Returning before the decider runs leaves it intact.
